WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.272 --> 00:00:09.369
Hi, this is a presentation on

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.673
‘Is it okay to pursue functional programming on the front end?’

00:00:12.674 --> 00:00:15.125
Let us begin the presentation

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.675
Hi, I’m working at Devsisters

00:00:18.676 --> 00:00:22.215
As a web front end developer, I am Kim Minsu

00:00:22.532 --> 00:00:26.336
I am interested in all technologies used at web front end

00:00:26.399 --> 00:00:29.255
Lately I’ve been interested in functional programming

00:00:30.935 --> 00:00:33.905
Hi, I am Lee Chang-Hee

00:00:34.449 --> 00:00:38.365
I did front and back-end developing at Devsisters

00:00:39.748 --> 00:00:43.810
Usually I contribute to JavaScript and Go

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.570
Functional programming,

00:00:47.571 --> 00:00:50.519
As the photo shows, I’m sure you think about things like

00:00:50.581 --> 00:00:54.705
Monad, category theory, and those complex things

00:00:55.075 --> 00:00:59.426
But in this presentation, I will refrain from going too deep into the theory

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.085
I will instead explain more on how it can be applied in reality

00:01:04.078 --> 00:01:07.859
Functional programming processes data as a pure function

00:01:08.003 --> 00:01:11.391
And it moves away from side effects as the programming paradigm

00:01:11.392 --> 00:01:12.775
I’m sure you know that much

00:01:13.629 --> 00:01:16.729
But you may be wondering that something at the front-end,

00:01:16.825 --> 00:01:19.285
DOM work, async data patching

00:01:19.635 --> 00:01:23.116
These actions may incur side effects,

00:01:23.117 --> 00:01:25.385
I’m sure you think that

00:01:26.411 --> 00:01:29.055
And it’s all very correct

00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:31.420
But functional programming,

00:01:31.421 --> 00:01:34.052
It is not that it uses no side effect at all

00:01:34.164 --> 00:01:37.705
It uses side effect and pure function at the same time

00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:39.795
And maintains them together

00:01:40.248 --> 00:01:44.452
And this program paradigm combines the functions generated here

00:01:45.971 --> 00:01:49.921
So how did we implement the functional programming to the team?

00:01:49.922 --> 00:01:51.495
Let me explain that now

00:01:51.847 --> 00:01:56.082
First, doing separate studies myself,

00:01:56.143 --> 00:02:00.055
I learned about the related theories with my personal studies

00:02:00.628 --> 00:02:03.451
And then in my actual project

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.825
I applied some simple functional methods

00:02:06.938 --> 00:02:09.223
To write the code

00:02:10.018 --> 00:02:14.485
And the teammates who were with me on the project saw that code

00:02:14.787 --> 00:02:16.570
And began studying with me

00:02:16.571 --> 00:02:19.775
So we started the functional programming inside the team

00:02:20.847 --> 00:02:24.065
And while we were studying within the team

00:02:24.309 --> 00:02:27.315
We had a case where we had to maintain

00:02:28.225 --> 00:02:29.905
Something complex as you see

00:02:31.365 --> 00:02:34.454
Four inputs for one form,

00:02:34.631 --> 00:02:37.775
And each form has separate verification logic

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:40.795
And in each different form

00:02:41.173 --> 00:02:44.185
We had some in-common verification processes as well

00:02:44.622 --> 00:02:49.195
Change-Hee will cover that more at the back in more depth

00:02:51.119 --> 00:02:53.859
Of many functional programming libraries

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:57.784
We chose fp-ts in our development

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.495
So why did we choose fp-ts?

00:03:01.115 --> 00:03:04.576
fp-ts supports something many functional programming languages support,

00:03:04.577 --> 00:03:06.445
Which is the type abstraction

00:03:07.126 --> 00:03:08.462
And these abstractions

00:03:08.463 --> 00:03:12.625
Follow the theories based on functional programming very well

00:03:13.488 --> 00:03:17.985
And there is a variety of library ecosystem that can be used together

00:03:18.889 --> 00:03:21.688
So before we go into actual cases

00:03:21.899 --> 00:03:24.951
Let me show you some needed type abstraction

00:03:24.952 --> 00:03:28.148
And utility functions

00:03:30.244 --> 00:03:33.138
Option type abstraction consists of

00:03:33.473 --> 00:03:36.718
None and Some type

00:03:37.128 --> 00:03:39.848
The code looks broken here, I don’t know why

00:03:40.212 --> 00:03:44.448
Option type is each container with the selective A type

00:03:45.047 --> 00:03:49.138
With the A type value, Option type is Some instance

00:03:50.291 --> 00:03:53.868
If there is no value, Option type is None instance

00:03:54.302 --> 00:03:58.568
Option type is the effect of calculation that can fail in general

00:03:58.954 --> 00:04:02.718
Now here are some examples of uses of Option type

00:04:04.892 --> 00:04:08.118
Option type can be brought in with the first line

00:04:09.395 --> 00:04:13.862
Using the Some type and None, we can create each type of instances

00:04:15.508 --> 00:04:19.224
Finding an index with a specific value in an array

00:04:19.225 --> 00:04:22.308
If there is no value for findindex function

00:04:22.309 --> 00:04:23.748
-1 is returned

00:04:24.366 --> 00:04:26.390
If the value-finding is successful

00:04:26.391 --> 00:04:29.108
The Some instance with the index value is returned

00:04:29.401 --> 00:04:32.678
If the value is not found, None instance is returned

00:04:33.552 --> 00:04:35.638
This is the actual use

00:04:35.829 --> 00:04:38.110
There is value 1 within the array

00:04:38.235 --> 00:04:39.298
Some tag

00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:42.942
And the object that has the 0 value for the array index

00:04:42.943 --> 00:04:45.064
Is returned as you see here

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:48.123
There is no value 4 within the array

00:04:48.124 --> 00:04:52.173
Second, we can see that the None instance with the None tag is returned

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.918
Also, with the fromNullable function

00:04:56.559 --> 00:04:58.678
We can create the Option type again

00:04:59.703 --> 00:05:01.228
fromNullable function

00:05:01.642 --> 00:05:05.228
When nullable value is sent to the argument, None instance is returned

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:08.028
Conversely, the non-nullable value

00:05:08.518 --> 00:05:10.991
If the not-nullable value is sent to the argument

00:05:10.992 --> 00:05:13.478
The Some instance with that value is returned

00:05:15.086 --> 00:05:19.558
And using the fromPredicate function, we can create the Option instance as well

00:05:20.175 --> 00:05:24.219
fromPredicacte has the function that returns Boolean as the argument

00:05:25.376 --> 00:05:29.155
IsNumber function is delivered to fromPredicate function

00:05:29.322 --> 00:05:31.518
To generate getOptionNumber function

00:05:32.834 --> 00:05:35.473
In the case of isNumber function returning true

00:05:35.474 --> 00:05:37.200
Some instance is returned

00:05:37.201 --> 00:05:41.188
If isNumber function returns false, None instance is returned

00:05:43.319 --> 00:05:44.841
Next, Either type

00:05:44.999 --> 00:05:47.378
Either type is similar to Option type

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:49.668
The two types express one value

00:05:50.345 --> 00:05:53.538
Either instance is Left and Right instance

00:05:54.125 --> 00:05:57.072
Either type, to process missing value

00:05:57.073 --> 00:05:58.678
It can be used in place of Option

00:05:59.035 --> 00:06:01.188
Option’s None instance

00:06:01.488 --> 00:06:04.628
Is replaced with Left instance that can include in the value

00:06:04.874 --> 00:06:08.343
In general, Left expresses failure

00:06:08.344 --> 00:06:10.178
And Right, success

00:06:10.948 --> 00:06:15.038
Let’s look at some examples of uses of Either type

00:06:16.782 --> 00:06:20.338
Either is often used in generating exceptions

00:06:20.762 --> 00:06:22.864
In this example

00:06:22.865 --> 00:06:25.558
I will use tryCatch function as well

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.548
It has something that turns text into JavaScript object

00:06:29.549 --> 00:06:32.148
The parse function converted to Either

00:06:32.717 --> 00:06:37.432
Using tryCatch, if the original parse function generates exception,

00:06:37.433 --> 00:06:39.578
It returns Left that contains error

00:06:40.175 --> 00:06:42.428
If parse function is realized well

00:06:42.666 --> 00:06:45.398
It returns Right that contains JavaScript object

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:50.048
Like the example, in the case of a successfully returned object,

00:06:50.043 --> 00:06:52.679
the object with the Right tag value

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.948
And Right property is returned

00:06:55.475 --> 00:06:56.933
For the exception

00:06:56.934 --> 00:07:00.808
The one with Left tag and value is returned

00:07:02.403 --> 00:07:04.716
For Either type, same as Option,

00:07:04.717 --> 00:07:07.648
fromNullable and fromPredicate can be used

00:07:08.558 --> 00:07:12.178
Some difference, for Either type, when Left is returned it gets

00:07:12.974 --> 00:07:15.258
The basic value for use as argument

00:07:17.186 --> 00:07:19.228
If nullable value is given

00:07:19.808 --> 00:07:23.678
The Left instance with the value from the above function is returned

00:07:24.134 --> 00:07:25.836
For the opposite case,

00:07:25.837 --> 00:07:28.628
Right instance with the value is returned well

00:07:29.958 --> 00:07:33.388
fromPredicate function is the same as fromNullable function

00:07:34.344 --> 00:07:38.223
When Left instance is returned, the value to be used

00:07:38.234 --> 00:07:41.278
is returned in the second argument additionally with the returning function

00:07:42.574 --> 00:07:44.469
When Left instance is returned

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:49.058
The function sent as the second argument to fromPredicate function is run

00:07:49.248 --> 00:07:50.778
The value is contained

00:07:50.779 --> 00:07:53.548
And the Left instance is returned

00:07:54.094 --> 00:07:57.027
If the function given to the first argument returns true

00:07:57.202 --> 00:08:00.168
The instance with the value is returned

00:08:01.847 --> 00:08:04.608
Next is the Task instance and Task type

00:08:04.883 --> 00:08:08.668
Task type expresses the fail-proof async calculation

00:08:09.073 --> 00:08:10.994
The async calculation that can fail

00:08:10.995 --> 00:08:13.362
Can use TaskEither that uses Either as well

00:08:13.363 --> 00:08:15.628
That I mentioned earlier

00:08:17.975 --> 00:08:19.607
Here is a written lead function that runs

00:08:19.608 --> 00:08:23.148
Async action with the user’s input

00:08:23.523 --> 00:08:25.985
Lead function, if the program is functioning

00:08:26.122 --> 00:08:29.418
It will keep on waiting for the user input and it won’t fail

00:08:32.279 --> 00:08:36.438
As the photo shows, we see how the lead function is working well

00:08:37.748 --> 00:08:40.530
TaskEither type, same as Either type

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:42.558
Can use the tryCatch function

00:08:44.274 --> 00:08:46.180
If the Promise is resolved

00:08:46.181 --> 00:08:49.458
The Right instance with the resolved value is returned

00:08:50.001 --> 00:08:54.113
Conversely for the reject, the function delivered to the second argument is run

00:08:54.363 --> 00:08:56.718
The return value of the second argument function

00:08:57.368 --> 00:09:00.087
which is the return value is returned in the Left instance

00:09:01.189 --> 00:09:02.720
It’s async function

00:09:02.721 --> 00:09:05.898
Using the async keyword it runs the function

00:09:07.843 --> 00:09:09.680
As I explained before

00:09:09.681 --> 00:09:13.408
We can see the returned instances for the right cases

00:09:15.206 --> 00:09:19.648
Next is fp-ts utility function called pipe

00:09:20.704 --> 00:09:23.686
As the example shows, there’s a currying function

00:09:23.857 --> 00:09:26.098
That adds the two numbers

00:09:26.322 --> 00:09:27.928
Using this add function

00:09:28.099 --> 00:09:31.108
I made the function that adds 1, 2, 3

00:09:31.548 --> 00:09:33.472
As the diagram shows

00:09:33.655 --> 00:09:36.489
If each function is run in a sequence

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:38.708
How can we call in the function?

00:09:39.983 --> 00:09:43.369
In general, from within the function call

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:46.318
We can fuse in the function as we call it in

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:51.518
However, what happens if there are more and more functions to fuse?

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:56.068
We can see how the code is getting really complicated

00:09:56.393 --> 00:09:58.266
To make the visuals more convenient

00:09:58.267 --> 00:10:01.348
What if it gains every time a function is called?

00:10:02.276 --> 00:10:05.458
I’m sure the same Callback Hell situation will occurs

00:10:07.407 --> 00:10:11.688
With the pipe function, the function can be fused simply like this

00:10:12.225 --> 00:10:16.288
It is much easier to follow how the function is fused

00:10:16.755 --> 00:10:21.027
We just need to read the value and function delivered as argument to pipe function

00:10:21.317 --> 00:10:23.691
From Left to right

00:10:24.543 --> 00:10:26.850
The utility function pipe

00:10:27.043 --> 00:10:29.801
Is suggested as JavaScript operator now

00:10:31.951 --> 00:10:35.451
How can we use the type abstractions we talked about before?

00:10:35.905 --> 00:10:37.843
To use type abstractions

00:10:37.844 --> 00:10:40.491
We need to know about a few functions that can be used together

00:10:40.804 --> 00:10:42.731
First is the map function

00:10:43.287 --> 00:10:46.711
It does the same thing as the map function at the JavaScript array

00:10:47.201 --> 00:10:50.781
And, map function is also called a mapping function

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:54.120
The map functions of type abstractions

00:10:54.121 --> 00:10:56.951
All receive the A type value

00:10:56.952 --> 00:11:00.701
And return B type value in the function it gets

00:11:02.221 --> 00:11:04.666
As the code states, for Some type

00:11:04.667 --> 00:11:08.071
The function delivered to Map function is run

00:11:08.773 --> 00:11:10.074
Following along the pipe

00:11:10.074 --> 00:11:13.281
String type, meeting with fromNullable function, became Option

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:16.116
And following the signature of the function delivered to map function

00:11:16.117 --> 00:11:18.331
We see how the type changes

00:11:20.182 --> 00:11:22.441
Conversely, with the None instance delivered

00:11:22.442 --> 00:11:25.551
The function delivered to the map function does not run

00:11:26.328 --> 00:11:29.948
Because the function delivered to fromPredicate returns false

00:11:30.085 --> 00:11:32.815
None instance is delivered

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:35.911
Meaning the result of the code is None

00:11:37.477 --> 00:11:39.331
Next is chain function

00:11:40.283 --> 00:11:44.221
Chain function is used to see whether to continue the calculation or not

00:11:44.632 --> 00:11:46.870
Using the result of the calculation before the operation

00:11:47.025 --> 00:11:49.171
The later calculation is done in sequence

00:11:49.561 --> 00:11:53.371
Let me talk about the Either type chain as the example

00:11:54.362 --> 00:11:56.190
Multipleby10 function

00:11:56.191 --> 00:11:59.921
Multiplies by 10 and returns instance if the input is a number

00:12:00.409 --> 00:12:02.107
If the input is not a number

00:12:02.108 --> 00:12:05.641
It returns the Left instance with the text

00:12:07.547 --> 00:12:11.108
Increment gets number and returns the Right instance that

00:12:11.109 --> 00:12:12.781
Contains the value that adds one

00:12:13.835 --> 00:12:16.621
With fusing this function you can make a new function

00:12:17.851 --> 00:12:21.141
The delivered value when this function was called is text

00:12:22.834 --> 00:12:26.101
So this multipleby10 function returns Left instance

00:12:26.523 --> 00:12:30.167
So, the increment function delivered to chain does not run

00:12:30.396 --> 00:12:34.911
Left instance is returns in sum

00:12:36.694 --> 00:12:39.964
Conversely, if the delivered value is number 10

00:12:39.998 --> 00:12:44.171
Multipleby10 function returns the Right instance with the number 10

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:47.431
Right instance is delivered to chain function

00:12:47.475 --> 00:12:50.788
So the increment function delivered to chain is called and we see

00:12:50.948 --> 00:12:53.691
That the Right instance with one added is returned

00:12:55.428 --> 00:12:58.971
Finally let me talk about the match and fold function

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:02.161
Match and fold serve the same function

00:13:02.878 --> 00:13:04.928
For Option type, Some and None

00:13:04.929 --> 00:13:07.740
Either type, Left and Right

00:13:07.741 --> 00:13:11.051
For each case, the function to run is delivered to run

00:13:12.525 --> 00:13:15.896
If the delivered value to pipe is not 0

00:13:15.897 --> 00:13:19.021
fromPredicate function returns Some instance

00:13:19.675 --> 00:13:22.392
If Some instance is delivered to match function

00:13:22.528 --> 00:13:25.071
The function delivered to the second argument is run

00:13:28.348 --> 00:13:31.046
If the first delivered value to pipe is 0

00:13:31.092 --> 00:13:33.801
FromPredicate function returns None instance

00:13:34.395 --> 00:13:36.842
If None instance is delivered to match function

00:13:36.843 --> 00:13:39.481
The function delivered to the first argument is run

00:13:40.086 --> 00:13:45.757
So that was it on some type abstractions, pipe function, and how to use

00:13:46.821 --> 00:13:49.690
The type abstraction

00:13:49.691 --> 00:13:54.511
So let’s now talk about real life examples that were resolved with this

00:13:57.221 --> 00:14:01.971
So something we talked about before,

00:14:01.972 --> 00:14:07.846
Using the abstraction data type, let’s see how we solved the actual problems

00:14:12.791 --> 00:14:17.049
As said, we were building a job recruiting website

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:24.551
And the form for application ended 8 input maintenance

00:14:26.691 --> 00:14:30.605
We first got the input from the user in the form

00:14:30.606 --> 00:14:35.416
And validate to see if the entered value is correct

00:14:35.417 --> 00:14:41.107
if it’s not validated, error message should be there

00:14:41.108 --> 00:14:44.262
That was the common problem for all

00:14:46.286 --> 00:14:51.110
If there’s too many fields the code looks ugly

00:14:51.111 --> 00:14:55.202
So let’s just consider one aspect, the phone number field

00:14:56.164 --> 00:14:59.658
If we’re to make this application form

00:14:59.659 --> 00:15:04.123
The state managing phone number value

00:15:04.124 --> 00:15:09.110
And the state with the error message with the issue with the phone number value

00:15:09.111 --> 00:15:11.179
These are the two states

00:15:12.396 --> 00:15:17.336
And there will be a logic that validates whether the phone number is correct

00:15:19.741 --> 00:15:24.860
And whenever the user changes the input, with onChange event

00:15:24.861 --> 00:15:29.644
It’ll keep the state value managed

00:15:31.106 --> 00:15:34.840
In form event toad let’s like form onSubmit

00:15:34.841 --> 00:15:39.751
Before the form submission it reviews the whole thing again

00:15:41.041 --> 00:15:45.445
The component data to show to the user

00:15:45.446 --> 00:15:50.043
Input from the user and the span with error messages

00:15:50.044 --> 00:15:52.646
It’s wrapped simply with span here

00:15:52.647 --> 00:15:57.731
Those dome objects will exist

00:15:59.009 --> 00:16:02.810
So let’s sum up what I said before

00:16:02.811 --> 00:16:07.125
To realized a field that can be validated

00:16:07.126 --> 00:16:11.130
Two states, one field validating function

00:16:11.131 --> 00:16:18.499
And the event handler function detecting field’s input value, we need these four

00:16:19.841 --> 00:16:24.730
But in fact, maintaining the eight inputs

00:16:24.731 --> 00:16:29.260
16 states should be maintained within the component

00:16:29.261 --> 00:16:34.081
Event handler and validating logic should be written separately

00:16:36.061 --> 00:16:39.597
Here I only tried three

00:16:39.598 --> 00:16:43.302
If it is realized with the say we mentioned

00:16:43.303 --> 00:16:46.060
This is how The component code looks like

00:16:47.321 --> 00:16:54.560
But we wanted to minimize the number of codes within the component

00:16:54.561 --> 00:16:59.350
So we thought on how to solve this issue

00:16:59.351 --> 00:17:06.422
I’m sure you’re thinking of the how yourself as you’re listening

00:17:07.424 --> 00:17:11.041
In fact, the library to resolve this issue

00:17:11.042 --> 00:17:15.657
Is already well made, like React-Hook-Form or Formik

00:17:15.659 --> 00:17:19.376
So most cases we use that

00:17:19.377 --> 00:17:25.272
But for us, there was a consensus within our team to try and apply

00:17:25.273 --> 00:17:28.813
The functional programming

00:17:28.814 --> 00:17:36.083
So the fp-ts library that we tried in other projects,

00:17:36.084 --> 00:17:38.488
We wanted to apply it here as well

00:17:40.514 --> 00:17:48.968
before solving the issue, using this fp-ts which should be rewritten?

00:17:49.664 --> 00:17:52.433
We analyzed the issue again

00:17:52.434 --> 00:17:57.360
First, we need two states for maintaining input value and error message

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:00.504
And refresh the states if the input is in

00:18:00.504 --> 00:18:06.631
Validate to see if the value is correct, if not, send error message

00:18:08.104 --> 00:18:14.532
But here, the object or realization for each field differs

00:18:14.533 --> 00:18:20.396
Only with the three: input value, error message and validating function

00:18:20.397 --> 00:18:26.093
Here, all other parts are something worked commonly on all fields

00:18:26.094 --> 00:18:29.267
So the input value, error message, validating function

00:18:29.268 --> 00:18:34.708
These three values, abstracting the code into the form of something exerted from outside

00:18:34.709 --> 00:18:39.791
We thought writing the code will be much easier

00:18:41.004 --> 00:18:49.033
So to solve that issue, we took four steps

00:18:49.034 --> 00:18:53.199
Here I will show you the codes we actually applied for production

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:58.300
To show you how it works and how it’s used in this use case

00:19:00.834 --> 00:19:06.078
First, we made the common validation function

00:19:07.594 --> 00:19:12.982
Validation function parameter has validator here

00:19:12.983 --> 00:19:16.805
The functions to validate each field

00:19:16.806 --> 00:19:21.670
And the error message for user when the validation fails

00:19:21.671 --> 00:19:30.109
And the return value of the function is the query function for the validated value

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:31.533
Those are the components

00:19:34.344 --> 00:19:38.578
Function signature is this currying function

00:19:39.644 --> 00:19:43.409
This function receives user input value

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:47.147
And it’s delivered to the function fromPredicate at Either

00:19:47.895 --> 00:19:55.511
This fromPredicate gets two functions as the first argument

00:19:55.512 --> 00:19:56.859
The first argument,

00:19:59.084 --> 00:20:07.303
It’s a logic that checks for some value that’s being delivered here

00:20:07.304 --> 00:20:12.445
So see, they get the value with the argument val

00:20:12.446 --> 00:20:14.918
This value is moved to validator

00:20:17.214 --> 00:20:22.678
So all inputs in validator through map function is moved to the function

00:20:22.679 --> 00:20:28.248
To see if this value is correct according to the rules

00:20:29.274 --> 00:20:30.144
So

00:20:30.129 --> 00:20:34.034
If this value is correct, this fromPredicate

00:20:34.035 --> 00:20:40.236
Returns the value the user input in Right type

00:20:40.237 --> 00:20:47.633
If not, it gets the result value of the function over to the second argument

00:20:47.634 --> 00:20:54.350
Here we will take the function’s result value, the value for the Left type as the error message

00:20:54.351 --> 00:20:59.836
So here the simple error message is returned in this written function

00:21:02.204 --> 00:21:05.107
Let’s see how it actually works

00:21:05.108 --> 00:21:12.591
Here, when the user input is correct phone number

00:21:12.592 --> 00:21:19.551
And a flawed phone number, each for Right and Left type

00:21:19.552 --> 00:21:24.903
The input and error message are shown

00:21:25.924 --> 00:21:34.102
And the validators that I talked about, are defined here

00:21:35.084 --> 00:21:39.874
It’s something I showed you at the simple example component

00:21:39.875 --> 00:21:46.893
In the validating function, if statement, validators, they are atomically divided

00:21:47.514 --> 00:21:52.873
For validation, there are rules used in one field

00:21:52.874 --> 00:21:57.737
But most cases it’s about the maximum or minimum length

00:21:57.738 --> 00:22:00.759
Or the limit to characters

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:04.001
Those are the commonly used rules

00:22:04.002 --> 00:22:07.012
So I wanted to make it reusable

00:22:07.907 --> 00:22:13.263
And let’s see how we use the rules above

00:22:13.264 --> 00:22:19.570
From the first to third, it’s numbers, starting with 01

00:22:19.571 --> 00:22:25.736
And it doesn’t go over 11 characters, all three are true

00:22:25.737 --> 00:22:31.042
But the last ‘minimum length 10’ rule

00:22:31.043 --> 00:22:33.902
It’s not met, so false is returned

00:22:36.414 --> 00:22:44.830
And then with the pre-made rules the validator for each field is made

00:22:45.804 --> 00:22:48.492
This validating function, overall, pipe

00:22:48.493 --> 00:22:52.813
Using the function pipe that I mentioned before

00:22:52.814 --> 00:22:59.918
Here, the phoneNumber number input field that the user fills in

00:22:59.919 --> 00:23:04.403
The user’s phone number is here first

00:23:04.404 --> 00:23:10.347
And towards validate function, the first-made

00:23:10.348 --> 00:23:14.072
Validation rules and error messages are moved over

00:23:16.114 --> 00:23:22.369
So this initial value is required in this form

00:23:22.370 --> 00:23:29.023
So is it over one character, meaning is there any input from the user?

00:23:29.024 --> 00:23:32.651
So if there is no input from the user

00:23:32.652 --> 00:23:37.533
‘It is required’ error message will be returned from Left type

00:23:37.534 --> 00:23:44.423
If something is returned, right type will be returned

00:23:44.424 --> 00:23:49.563
So the returned Either, Left or Right type

00:23:49.564 --> 00:23:55.914
It moves to the Either chain function argument right after

00:23:55.915 --> 00:24:01.658
Here, chain function gets Either type as the argument

00:24:01.659 --> 00:24:04.603
If the received value is Left

00:24:04.604 --> 00:24:10.316
Then it doesn’t work, it returns the input Left type as it is

00:24:10.317 --> 00:24:16.514
If it’s Right type, then the validate function sent to chain as argument

00:24:16.515 --> 00:24:20.832
To that function, Right type value is moved over as argument again

00:24:23.745 --> 00:24:28.344
Here’s a simple example for you

00:24:28.345 --> 00:24:34.004
No value is added, Left type’s ‘it is required’ error message

00:24:34.005 --> 00:24:42.386
The wrong value for the second validate

00:24:42.387 --> 00:24:45.489
Will now say ‘wrong format of numbers’

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:47.817
In the left type for the error message

00:24:47.818 --> 00:24:50.547
If it’s all passed

00:24:50.547 --> 00:24:55.740
The first phone number input from the user will be returned with the Right type

00:24:59.195 --> 00:25:03.469
Yes, and everything until now

00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:09.499
As a developer, to use it more conveniently, I made some custom hook

00:25:12.475 --> 00:25:17.804
The custom hook we made has the name useStateWithValidator

00:25:17.805 --> 00:25:23.700
As the name states, overall the interface is very similar to that of userState

00:25:26.215 --> 00:25:30.005
This hook has two internal state, which are

00:25:30.715 --> 00:25:35.564
As at the naive component in the beginning did,

00:25:35.565 --> 00:25:40.974
The value maintaining state and error message maintaining state, the two are there

00:25:42.225 --> 00:25:47.376
And here changeError, setting the error message

00:25:47.377 --> 00:25:51.715
A function similar to setState is wrapped here

00:25:51.716 --> 00:25:55.599
The return part for the error message as input

00:25:55.600 --> 00:26:02.988
I’ll explain when I talk about StateValidator object below

00:26:06.615 --> 00:26:16.254
Yes, and changeValue is a function similar to setState sending from inside to outside

00:26:16.255 --> 00:26:22.214
Delivering new value to the validator to see if it’s the right value

00:26:23.125 --> 00:26:29.314
So it’ll check whether left or right type is returned

00:26:29.315 --> 00:26:33.303
And using Either’s match function

00:26:33.304 --> 00:26:38.649
It will work differently for Left and Right type

00:26:40.365 --> 00:26:45.775
Either match function has two functions as arguments

00:26:45.776 --> 00:26:50.795
First, if the either as argument is left, the function to run

00:26:50.796 --> 00:26:53.677
First, if the either as argument is right, the function to run

00:26:54.236 --> 00:27:00.574
For left, function provided by  identity fp-ts is used

00:27:00.575 --> 00:27:06.826
For identity, it literally returns the value moved over as the function’s argument

00:27:06.827 --> 00:27:10.284
Right back, it’s a very simple function

00:27:10.285 --> 00:27:17.407
Here the Left is always the error message

00:27:17.408 --> 00:27:20.105
So here, it'd be good to understand that

00:27:20.106 --> 00:27:23.890
the input error message that's been received is being returned right away

00:27:26.081 --> 00:27:28.131
And for the Right type

00:27:30.091 --> 00:27:39.000
It gets the input and does setState and returns the empty text

00:27:43.701 --> 00:27:49.992
If there is an error message the string for the error message will arrive

00:27:49.993 --> 00:27:55.653
If there is no issue, then the empty text I talked about

00:27:55.654 --> 00:28:00.404
For the right type, the empty text will be returned

00:28:00.405 --> 00:28:02.161
So it will move over

00:28:07.741 --> 00:28:11.825
So finally with the moved over text

00:28:14.431 --> 00:28:17.019
The error is set up

00:28:20.571 --> 00:28:24.559
And we made an object called StateValidation separately

00:28:24.561 --> 00:28:31.355
This isn’t something that changes the state every time the value changes

00:28:35.181 --> 00:28:40.227
Like submitting a format, manually at a desired timing

00:28:40.228 --> 00:28:43.793
Form validation should be done in some cases

00:28:43.794 --> 00:28:48.121
And the validation function that can be used as a manual

00:28:48.122 --> 00:28:54.547
And the value that can allow the user to see for the errors on field

00:28:54.548 --> 00:28:57.791
It has the getter that can bring all that easily

00:29:03.171 --> 00:29:07.260
I am running out of time, so I will move a little quickly

00:29:07.261 --> 00:29:13.143
So with all four validating rules  we made

00:29:13.144 --> 00:29:19.594
Let’s see how we improved the existing code; the highlighted parts

00:29:21.711 --> 00:29:26.667
Here, using the field validating rules and custom hook

00:29:26.668 --> 00:29:29.540
It can be a one line code

00:29:29.541 --> 00:29:36.552
And change the value, validate at onChange event handle

00:29:36.553 --> 00:29:39.922
Validate, set the error message, those things

00:29:39.923 --> 00:29:45.120
It’s all internally processed in our custom hook

00:29:45.121 --> 00:29:51.440
So it’s very simple unless we need some other special work

00:29:51.441 --> 00:29:55.296
And for onSubmit, for that validation step

00:29:55.297 --> 00:29:59.626
We used to add the value of each field to the field’s validator

00:29:59.627 --> 00:30:02.810
To manually call them in

00:30:02.811 --> 00:30:10.160
Here, the interface is all the same with the validation function at StateValidator

00:30:10.161 --> 00:30:15.308
So only with the array of StateValidator it’s

00:30:15.309 --> 00:30:19.412
Easier to manually validate all these fields

00:30:21.491 --> 00:30:25.863
And for the error message the cited error state before

00:30:27.286 --> 00:30:30.601
Here, using error getter at StateValidator

00:30:30.602 --> 00:30:34.542
It’s not inside the component

00:30:34.543 --> 00:30:40.113
But the user can just get the value and use it, that’s the change

